<BaseLayout title="Methodology — how Freighthub researches and verifies" description="Freighthub's editorial method: source hierarchy, pre-publication checks, how ranges and simulations are labelled, and what is automated.">
  <article>
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="article-meta">Editorial standards · Last reviewed 15 November 2026</p>

    <p>How Freighthub is researched, written, reviewed and corrected — and how much weight to give each kind of claim.</p>

    <h2>1. The question comes first</h2>
    <p>Every page starts with the decision a shipper faces: which port, which mode, who clears, what certificate, how much, how long, what goes wrong. The page is organised around that decision, not a template, which is why the H2 order differs between countries and ports.</p>

    <h2>2. Three kinds of statement</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reported</strong> — a fact from an official source, port authority, regulator or trade press, cited at the foot of the page.</li>
      <li><strong>Industry claim</strong> — a range or practice attributed to forwarders, carriers or importers, labelled as a claim.</li>
      <li><strong>Editorial judgement</strong> — our reading of the evidence: labelled, arguable, never a regulator's position.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The labels tell the reader what can be checked and what is opinion.</p>

    <h2>3. Source hierarchy</h2>
    <p>When sources conflict, we resolve in this order; an official document beats any description of it.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Primary official documents.</strong> Customs regulations, tariff schedules and e-service guidance; standards and their scope; port tariffs; tax filing rules; IMO and IATA instruments — read in the original where the desk can.</li>
      <li><strong>Peer-reviewed and institutional research.</strong> Academic work on trade logistics, and World Bank, UNCTAD, ITC or WTO datasets: used for baselines and direction, rarely for a live price.</li>
      <li><strong>Industry publications.</strong> Carrier and terminal notices, recognised trade press, freight indices — reliable on what is happening, weaker on why, often wrong on rules.</li>
      <li><strong>Industry commentary.</strong> Forwarder guides and practitioner posts, treated as a claim.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>4. What is never used as a sole source</h2>
    <p>Useful for orientation, never the only basis for a published claim:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>encyclopaedias and their citations;</li>
      <li>AI-generated summaries, answer engines and chatbot output;</li>
      <li>commercial content sites, competitor guides, forums and social posts;</li>
      <li>undated rate cards, brochures and a company's own press release;</li>
      <li>quotation screenshots, figures with no establishable date, and a single anonymous source.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each can be right; none can be checked.</p>

    <h2>5. The verification steps before publication</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Claim inventory</strong> — every assertion listed against a source; the source is opened, not recalled, and must say what the draft claims.</li>
      <li><strong>Currency</strong> — is the rule still in force, or superseded?</li>
      <li><strong>Second check on numbers</strong> — anything a reader might budget against is confirmed independently, or published as a range with its basis.</li>
      <li><strong>Label and range check</strong> — as-of date, shipment assumed, inclusions and drivers present; fact, claim and judgement distinguishable.</li>
      <li><strong>Consistency</strong> — the page agrees with the pages it links to, or says why it differs.</li>
      <li><strong>Link check</strong> — each external link resolves to the document described.</li>
      <li><strong>Sign-off</strong> — the person who publishes answers for it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If a step fails the claim is removed, not softened: a hedged sentence nobody can support is still unsupported.</p>

    <h2>6. How ranges are set and labelled</h2>
    <p>Ranges are built, not remembered: a base port-to-port or airport-to-airport band at a stated date, plus origin handling, destination charges, duty and VAT where they apply, the inland leg, and a buffer for the known failure modes on that lane. Bands come from published indices or carrier rate levels, add-ons from tariff schedules.</p>
    <p>Every range states when it was built, the shipment it assumes (usually one 40ft container, port-to-port, or one cubic metre for LCL), what is included, and what would move it most. It is labelled <em>planning range, not a quotation</em>, and a range over two quarters old is flagged.</p>

    <h2>7. Worked examples and simulations</h2>
    <p>Some pages use a worked example or simulation: a constructed shipment showing how a decision plays out, labelled where it appears with its assumptions — commodity, value, volume, route, Incoterm, month — and which figures are illustrative.</p>
    <p>A simulation is not evidence. It describes no real shipment, company or forwarder price, and must not be cited as a benchmark. Its job is to make a mechanism visible: how duty stacks on freight, where a demurrage clock starts.</p>

    <h2>8. Language, translation and terminology</h2>
    <p>Most primary documents here are Arabic or Chinese. They are read in the original where the desk can; where it cannot, the page says which translation it relied on, and an official English version is used where one exists.</p>
    <p>First use of a foreign term gives the original with a plain-English gloss. Official English names are preferred to translations of convenience; where a rendering is contested, the page uses the authority's own and notes the variant. HS codes are given at heading level unless a subheading is the point.</p>

    <h2>9. What we will not publish</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Precise freight rates presented as current prices.</li>
      <li>Guaranteed transit times.</li>
      <li>Client names or case studies that identify a company without consent.</li>
      <li>Certifications, awards or credentials we do not hold.</li>
      <li>Invented sources, dates or quotations.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>10. How the pages are built</h2>
    <p>A major page normally carries a direct answer in the first hundred words, a key-facts table, cost and transit drivers as ranges, failure modes, a labelled judgement, an FAQ, sources, an update log and related links. Not every page uses every module: a comparison leads with a verdict, a port page with the terminal.</p>

    <h2>11. Automation: what is checked by machine, and what is not</h2>
    <p>Freighthub does not run an automated fact-checker. No tool reads a page and confirms its claims, and nothing is published because a model was confident.</p>
    <p>Automation is narrow and mechanical: research support (finding documents a person then opens), drafting and copy-editing, and checks on form — a failed build, missing metadata, a malformed table, a bad date format, a dead link. Those catch presentation defects; they cannot tell whether a sentence is true.</p>
    <p>Every step in section 5 is done by a person, and so are the judgement calls; the desk is accountable for what is published and a model is not. A sentence published without verification is a defect.</p>

    <h2>12. Dates, review cadence and updates</h2>
    <p>Review follows how fast the facts move, not a single calendar:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Country and port pages</strong> — at least twice a year, and within thirty days of a change to a tariff, port rule or service.</li>
      <li><strong>Method guides and sector pages</strong> — at least annually, or when a standard or process changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Policy pages</strong> — <a href="/about/">about</a>, corrections, privacy and terms, and this page: at least annually.</li>
      <li><strong>Updates</strong> — dated entries as events happen; earlier entries are not rewritten.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A review is recorded as a change to the <em>last reviewed</em> date: the page was checked for accuracy, stale ranges and broken links, not rewritten. The <a href="/updates/">updates feed</a> states what changed, why it matters and what to do.</p>

    <h2>13. Corrections</h2>
    <p>Errors are corrected on the page and logged in <a href="/corrections/">corrections</a>: a correction is a factual fix, a clarification a wording fix, an update new information. We do not silently delete pages to avoid a correction.</p>

    <h2>14. What this site is not, and how to reach it</h2>
    <p>Freighthub is not a forwarder, broker, carrier or marketplace. The advice is general and should be checked against current regulations and the specific shipment; the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a> sets out those limits.</p>
    <p>Questions, corrections and source suggestions go to the <a href="/contact/">editorial desk</a>. Personal data is covered by the <a href="/privacy/">privacy notice</a>; reuse of the text by the <a href="/terms/">terms</a>.</p>
